>
> I agree. "I have read this and owe nothing" is your judgment, and no
> hook can derive it from the list. It is also yours alone; my inbox
> should not change when you archive something. The shared archive/
> folder from "[PATCH v2 0/2] Archive messages that need no answer" [1]
> got that wrong, since one move closed the message for everyone. It also
> needs a shared maildir, which real email will not give you. I would
> drop that series.
>
> notmuch-git(1) fits, as you suggested in "Re: need some way to close
> the loop on threads" [2]. The shape I would propose:
>
> - One personal tag under a prefix, e.g. "me::archived". notmuch-git
>   commits only tags matching git.tag_prefix, so the derived tags
>   (inbox, replied, superseded, ...) never enter it, and checkout never
>   fights mail-tag.sh over them.
> - mail-tag.sh treats it as an input, the way it treats your address:
>   "-inbox -- tag:me::archived". Today it re-adds inbox on every run,
>   which is why "a" in emacs never sticks. That one line is the only
>   change to the derived tags, and it keeps inbox exclusive of archived.
> - It works per message, so a new reply in an archived thread still
>   lands in your inbox.
> - The repo stores one empty file per tag, tags/<msgid>/<tag>, and
>   knows nothing else about the mail. It can be a bare repo on your
>   machine, pushed to a branch of lab.git for backup, e.g. tags/pat.
>   Nobody else reads it, so it never merges.
>
> The archive is then "notmuch tag +me::archived" (or "a" in emacs)
> followed by "notmuch git commit". Rebuilding the database loses
> nothing: notmuch new derives the shared tags, and "notmuch git
> checkout" restores yours. No service is involved, only a git repo.

Is me a notmuch convention, or something built in, or just something you
came up with? Because I wonder how this would work with multiple
people. Imagine I'm working with a team of people (or agents) and
everyone has their own archive tag. How does that work? Does me
automatically resolve to the current user? Or would we need individual
prefixes like pat, claude, etc. And if we do that - can we stop those
additional tag prefixes from polluting the UI? I don't care to see
claude tags.

